1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,870
Hold an object up to a lamp,
and it throws a shadow on the wall.

2
00:00:05,710 --> 00:00:08,150
Turn it a little, and the shadow changes.

3
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:12,610
From most sides,
this one looks like a shapeless blob.

4
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:14,820
But from one direction,

5
00:00:14,870 --> 00:00:18,620
three separate groups appear,
with a spiral running through them.

6
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:21,800
A shadow is flat.

7
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:24,440
It always throws a dimension away.

8
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:28,520
The question is which view
throws away the least.

9
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:32,690
Data has the same problem, only worse.

10
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:35,480
Take a scan of a handwritten digit.

11
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:40,770
It's a small grid of sixty-four squares,
each one some shade of gray.

12
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:45,960
Write those shades down in a row,
and one digit becomes sixty-four numbers.

13
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:49,200
Two numbers, we can plot on a page.

14
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,780
Three, we can turn in our hands.

15
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:55,553
Sixty-four, nobody can picture.

16
00:00:56,007 --> 00:00:59,170
And this table has almost
eighteen hundred rows.

17
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,380
So let's ask the data for its best shadow.

18
00:01:03,970 --> 00:01:06,230
Squeeze every digit down from sixty-four

19
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,990
numbers to just two,
and plot each one as a dot.

20
00:01:11,670 --> 00:01:14,370
Nobody told the method
which digit is which.

21
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:17,550
And still the zeros gather in one place,

22
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,850
the sixes in another,
the fours somewhere else.

23
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:26,010
Two numbers out of sixty-four,
and much of the structure survives.

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:32,080
The method that found this view is called
principal component analysis, or PCA.

25
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:35,630
It shows up all over science.

26
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:38,390
Feed it the genes of people across Europe,

27
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:41,640
and its best shadow looks
a lot like a map of Europe.

28
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:44,327
Feed it photos of faces,

29
00:01:44,353 --> 00:01:49,927
and it finds a small set of ghostly base
faces that every face is built from.

30
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:53,340
It shrinks images
and cleans up noisy signals.

31
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:58,360
And in finance, it finds that interest
rates mostly move in just three ways:

32
00:01:58,410 --> 00:02:02,170
they rise together,
they tilt, or they bend.

33
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:04,810
Whenever there are too many

34
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:09,180
measurements to look at, PCA is one
of the first tools people reach for.

35
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,140
The idea is older than computers.

36
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:14,780
In nineteen oh one,

37
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:19,240
Karl Pearson looked for the line
that passes closest to a cloud of points.

38
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,400
In nineteen thirty three,
Harold Hotelling looked for the directions

39
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:27,810
in which data varies the most,
and he named them principal components.

40
00:02:28,450 --> 00:02:30,980
They turned out to be the same answer.

41
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:36,490
We'll build it one step at a time:
data as a cloud of points,

42
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:40,600
the best direction,
one matrix, its eigenvectors,

43
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:43,930
cutting the dimensions down,
and where it all breaks.

44
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:48,510
By the end, that map of digits
will make complete sense.

45
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:52,110
Let's start small.

46
00:02:52,390 --> 00:02:55,850
Forty people,
and two measurements each: height,

47
00:02:55,900 --> 00:03:01,150
and arm span, the distance from fingertip
to fingertip with arms stretched out.

48
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,190
Each row of the table is one person.

49
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:09,730
Give each person a dot, with height along
one axis and arm span along the other.

50
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,400
The dots don't scatter at random.

51
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:18,080
They lean along a diagonal, because
taller people tend to have longer arms.

52
00:03:18,590 --> 00:03:22,390
Leonardo da Vinci noticed this more
than five hundred years ago:

53
00:03:22,470 --> 00:03:25,550
for most people,
arm span is close to height.

54
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,630
The first step is to find
the centre of the cloud.

55
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:33,537
Average all the heights,
average all the arm spans,

56
00:03:33,587 --> 00:03:36,510
and you get one point: the average person.

57
00:03:37,410 --> 00:03:41,750
Then slide the whole cloud
so the average person sits at zero.

58
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:44,850
Nothing about the shape changes.

59
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:49,360
We only stop caring where the cloud is,
so we can study how it's spread out.

60
00:03:50,530 --> 00:03:51,980
How spread out is it?

61
00:03:52,730 --> 00:03:54,160
Take each person's height,

62
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:59,500
see how far it is from the average height,
square that gap, and average the squares.

63
00:03:59,930 --> 00:04:02,250
That number is the variance.

64
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,410
Squaring makes every

65
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:09,010
gap count as positive, and it makes far
points count much more than near ones.

66
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:13,970
We can measure the spread along height,
and along arm span.

67
00:04:14,370 --> 00:04:16,140
But look along the diagonal.

68
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:19,860
The cloud stretches further
that way than along either one.

69
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:22,390
The directions we happened to measure

70
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:25,350
in are not the directions
the data cares about.

71
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,570
Add a third measurement,

72
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:33,200
say shoulder width, and every person
becomes a point in three dimensions.

73
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:38,030
The cloud is still there.
We just need to turn it to see its shape.

74
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:40,970
The digits work the same way.

75
00:04:41,210 --> 00:04:43,190
Sixty-four shades in a column

76
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:46,630
make one point in a space
with sixty-four directions.

77
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:49,530
We can't turn that cloud to look at it.

78
00:04:49,870 --> 00:04:54,030
So we need a way to find its best
view by calculation instead.

79
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:59,200
Back to two dimensions,
where we can see everything.

80
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:01,717
Draw a line through the centre,

81
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:05,130
and drop every point straight onto it,
at a right angle.

82
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:09,430
Those landing spots are the shadows
of the points on this line.

83
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:12,150
Now turn the line.

84
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,780
Pointing one way,
the shadows bunch up together.

85
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:19,413
Pointing another, they spread far apart.

86
00:05:20,067 --> 00:05:22,980
Let's record the spread
of the shadows for every angle.

87
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:27,090
The record has one clear peak.

88
00:05:27,590 --> 00:05:31,930
That direction keeps the most spread,
and it's the first principal component.

89
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:35,650
There's a second way to pick a best line.

90
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:39,467
Pearson wanted the line
that passes closest to the points,

91
00:05:39,493 --> 00:05:42,030
with the smallest total of squared gaps.

92
00:05:43,070 --> 00:05:47,280
Look at one point.
Its distance from the centre is fixed.

93
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,020
Its shadow and its gap

94
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:53,350
to the line make a right triangle,
with that distance as the long side.

95
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:57,050
By Pythagoras,
the shadow squared and the gap

96
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,670
squared always add up to the same thing.

97
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:03,950
So when the shadows grow,
the gaps must shrink.

98
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:08,340
The line with the most spread
is the line that fits closest.

99
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:11,960
Pearson's line and Hotelling's
direction are the same.

100
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:17,910
To compute this, describe the direction
with an arrow one unit long.

101
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:22,770
A point's shadow on that line is the dot
product of the point with the arrow.

102
00:06:23,490 --> 00:06:27,290
Square each shadow and average
over all the points, and you get

103
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,250
the variance in that direction.

104
00:06:30,450 --> 00:06:33,390
So the whole problem fits in one sentence.

105
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:37,970
Among all arrows of length one,
find the one that makes this average

106
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,610
as big as possible.

107
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:43,800
One direction rarely
tells the whole story.

108
00:06:44,130 --> 00:06:46,370
Look for the best direction again,

109
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:49,450
but only among those at a right
angle to the first.

110
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:52,190
That's the second principal component.

111
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:55,240
It picks up the spread
the first one missed.

112
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:01,070
Turn the grid to line up with these two,
and every person gets new coordinates.

113
00:07:01,570 --> 00:07:05,450
Along the first axis,
people differ in overall size.

114
00:07:05,850 --> 00:07:09,830
Along the second, in whether their arms
are long or short for their height.

115
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:15,450
Same people, same cloud,
but the axes finally mean something.

116
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:20,700
Searching every angle
works in two dimensions.

117
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:24,720
In sixty-four,
there are far too many directions to try.

118
00:07:25,470 --> 00:07:27,470
We need the answer directly.

119
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:31,890
Start from the formula
for the variance along an arrow.

120
00:07:32,470 --> 00:07:36,200
Write each shadow as a small
matrix product, and regroup.

121
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:39,910
The arrow moves to the outside,
and everything about

122
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:41,810
the data collects in the middle.

123
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:46,980
That middle part is a single matrix,
built once from the data.

124
00:07:47,330 --> 00:07:49,910
It's called the covariance matrix.

125
00:07:50,490 --> 00:07:53,680
Once we have it,
we never need to touch the points again.

126
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:56,390
What's inside it?

127
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:59,090
Down the diagonal sit the variances we

128
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:02,860
already met: the spread of height,
and the spread of arm span.

129
00:08:03,890 --> 00:08:07,450
Off the diagonal sits something new,
the covariance.

130
00:08:07,830 --> 00:08:11,260
For each person, take how far
they are from the average height,

131
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:14,850
and multiply it by how far they
are from the average arm span.

132
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:18,890
A tall person with long arms
gives a positive product.

133
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:21,640
So does a short person with short arms.

134
00:08:22,050 --> 00:08:24,230
Mixed cases give negative ones.

135
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:26,540
Average the products.

136
00:08:26,830 --> 00:08:31,490
A large positive covariance says the two
measurements tend to rise together.

137
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,670
And the matrix is symmetric,
because height with arm

138
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:37,340
span is the same as arm span with height.

139
00:08:38,970 --> 00:08:41,830
A matrix is also a motion.

140
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:45,540
Apply the covariance matrix
to every arrow on a circle,

141
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:49,450
and the circle stretches into an ellipse,
tilted along the cloud.

142
00:08:50,750 --> 00:08:53,590
And remember the spread we
recorded for every angle?

143
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:58,890
Measure how far each stretched arrow
reaches along its own original direction.

144
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:03,030
That's the same record,
rebuilt from the matrix alone.

145
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:07,460
The matrix knows the variance
in every direction.

146
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:14,200
For the digits, the covariance matrix has
sixty-four rows and sixty-four columns,

147
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:15,850
one for each pixel.

148
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:20,550
Each entry says how strongly
two pixels brighten together.

149
00:09:21,370 --> 00:09:23,170
Neighbouring pixels mostly rise

150
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:27,020
and fall together, because a pen
stroke is wider than one square.

151
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:32,790
A few pixels near the edges are blank
in every scan, so their rows are empty.

152
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:37,780
Somewhere in this grid of numbers
is the best view of the digits.

153
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:43,550
Watch what the covariance
matrix does to a fan of arrows.

154
00:09:44,490 --> 00:09:48,360
Almost every arrow gets stretched
and turned off its own line.

155
00:09:49,030 --> 00:09:51,390
But two directions only stretch.

156
00:09:51,810 --> 00:09:54,200
They stay pointing
exactly where they were.

157
00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:59,800
Arrows like these are called eigenvectors,
from the German word for own.

158
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:04,780
Each one has a number that says how
much it's stretched, its eigenvalue.

159
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:09,820
And these two lie right along
the long and short axes of our cloud.

160
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:14,310
Why should the best
direction be an eigenvector?

161
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:19,010
Describe any arrow using
the two eigenvectors as axes.

162
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,790
Its variance becomes a mix
of the two eigenvalues,

163
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:25,150
with weights that always add up to one.

164
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:29,330
A mix can never be bigger
than its largest ingredient.

165
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:34,060
So the best you can do is put all
the weight on the largest eigenvalue.

166
00:10:34,410 --> 00:10:37,430
That means pointing straight
along its eigenvector.

167
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:43,140
The method of Lagrange multipliers
reaches the same place: at the best arrow,

168
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:46,810
the matrix times the arrow
points along the arrow itself.

169
00:10:48,270 --> 00:10:51,060
So each eigenvalue has a plain meaning.

170
00:10:51,310 --> 00:10:54,650
It's the variance of the data
along its eigenvector.

171
00:10:55,530 --> 00:10:59,430
And because the covariance matrix
is symmetric, its eigenvectors

172
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:01,360
always meet at right angles.

173
00:11:01,990 --> 00:11:06,550
That's why the principal components come
out perpendicular, with no extra work.

174
00:11:07,620 --> 00:11:11,400
Put it together, and the matrix
splits into three simple moves:

175
00:11:11,550 --> 00:11:16,530
turn to the eigenvector axes,
stretch by the eigenvalues, and turn back.

176
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,780
That's the whole method.

177
00:11:20,390 --> 00:11:21,750
Centre the data.

178
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:24,140
Build the covariance matrix.

179
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:26,350
Find its eigenvectors.

180
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:29,630
Sort them by eigenvalue, largest first.

181
00:11:29,830 --> 00:11:33,930
Then describe every point
by its shadows on the top few.

182
00:11:35,170 --> 00:11:37,150
No searching, no guessing.

183
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:42,470
The same steps work for two columns,
for sixty-four, or for ten thousand.

184
00:11:44,630 --> 00:11:47,390
Back to our people,
with three measurements.

185
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:52,450
Their cloud is long, fairly wide,
and quite thin, like a pebble.

186
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,590
The first two principal components span
a flat sheet through the middle of it.

187
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:02,330
Drop every point onto that sheet,
and turn the sheet to face us.

188
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:06,630
We've gone from three
numbers per person to two.

189
00:12:07,320 --> 00:12:11,220
What we threw away is only the short
drop from each point to the sheet.

190
00:12:12,950 --> 00:12:14,560
How much did we lose?

191
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:16,970
The eigenvalues answer that.

192
00:12:17,250 --> 00:12:19,030
Each one is the variance

193
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:23,250
along its direction, and together
they add up to the total variance.

194
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:27,160
So each eigenvalue's share
of the total is the share

195
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:29,670
of the variation that direction carries.

196
00:12:30,940 --> 00:12:35,090
For the digits, the first two directions
carry a little more than a quarter.

197
00:12:35,660 --> 00:12:38,130
About twenty of them carry nine tenths.

198
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:41,740
And three carry nothing at all:
those are the pixels

199
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:43,730
that are blank in every scan.

200
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:47,350
Each principal component of the digits

201
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:52,150
is itself a pattern of sixty-four shades,
so we can draw it as an image.

202
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:57,310
Every digit is the average digit
plus some amount of each pattern.

203
00:12:58,020 --> 00:13:02,480
Start from the average, and add
the patterns one at a time, largest first.

204
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:05,580
With a few, it's a blur.

205
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:07,860
With ten, you can read it.

206
00:13:08,450 --> 00:13:11,150
With twenty,
it's hard to tell from the original.

207
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:16,590
So twenty numbers per digit,
instead of sixty-four, lose very little.

208
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,660
Now the map from the beginning
makes sense.

209
00:13:20,990 --> 00:13:24,630
Its two axes are the first
two principal components.

210
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:30,420
Each dot is one digit, placed by how
much of each pattern it contains.

211
00:13:31,180 --> 00:13:35,480
Digits that share strokes contain similar
amounts, so they land near each other.

212
00:13:36,750 --> 00:13:40,740
In practice, nobody builds
the covariance matrix for large data.

213
00:13:41,410 --> 00:13:45,020
A related factorization,
the singular value decomposition,

214
00:13:45,060 --> 00:13:48,970
finds the same directions straight
from the table, and more accurately.

215
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,570
The answer is the same:
the directions of greatest spread.

216
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:57,180
PCA is simple,

217
00:13:57,220 --> 00:13:59,530
and that makes its limits easy to see.

218
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:04,580
Suppose we record height in millimetres,
but arm span in metres.

219
00:14:05,090 --> 00:14:09,480
The height numbers are now a thousand
times bigger than the arm span numbers,

220
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:12,520
so their variance
is a million times bigger.

221
00:14:13,250 --> 00:14:14,910
The first component points almost

222
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:17,610
straight along height,
whatever the people are like.

223
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:20,390
The fix is to put every

224
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:25,030
measurement on the same footing first:
divide each one by its own spread.

225
00:14:26,450 --> 00:14:29,520
PCA also only looks for flat views.

226
00:14:30,110 --> 00:14:33,760
Roll a sheet of points into a spiral,
like a cinnamon roll.

227
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:38,017
Its best flat shadow squashes
the layers on top of each other,

228
00:14:38,067 --> 00:14:41,790
and points that were far apart
along the sheet land side by side.

229
00:14:42,870 --> 00:14:44,310
For curved data like this,

230
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:50,070
people use methods that follow the curve,
such as kernel PCA, t-SNE and UMAP.

231
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:54,590
And the biggest spread is not
always the most interesting.

232
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:59,800
Take two groups that are long and thin,
lying side by side.

233
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,910
Most of the spread runs along
their length, so that's where

234
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:05,280
the first component points.

235
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:08,510
Its shadow piles both
groups on top of each other.

236
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:14,990
The direction that separates them has
very little spread, and PCA ranks it last.

237
00:15:16,070 --> 00:15:19,700
PCA finds the view that keeps
the most variation.

238
00:15:19,990 --> 00:15:22,910
Whether that's the view you
need is still your call.

239
00:15:25,610 --> 00:15:27,620
Let's put it all back together.

240
00:15:28,210 --> 00:15:30,690
Data becomes a cloud of points.

241
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:35,340
We centre it, and ask which direction
spreads the shadows the most.

242
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:39,370
That variance fits inside
one symmetric matrix.

243
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:41,840
Its eigenvectors are the answer,

244
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:45,030
and its eigenvalues say
how much each one keeps.

245
00:15:45,740 --> 00:15:49,220
Keep the top few,
and sixty-four numbers become a handful,

246
00:15:49,270 --> 00:15:51,380
with most of the picture still there.

247
00:15:53,110 --> 00:15:56,920
Every view of data throws something away,
just like a shadow.

248
00:15:57,680 --> 00:15:59,690
PCA doesn't avoid that.

249
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:04,513
It tells you exactly what you kept,
and exactly what you lost.

250
00:16:05,167 --> 00:16:10,100
So the next time you meet a table with too
many columns, turn it to the light first.
